+# Fifth sample — 2026-10-14T08:15Z (all-seat ballot under event-driven regime)
+
+Context: model-compliance-audit published for vote (sitting 3); clerk expanded the
+summons to all nine seats for the ballot. Two samples inside one minute:
+
+| Metric | 08:14:18Z | 08:15:54Z |
+|---|---|---|
+| cgroup `memory.current` | 7.68 GiB (96%) | 7.29 GiB (91%) |
+| Seat processes (RSS) | 9 (5.16 GB) | 8 (4.12 GB) |
+| Load avg (1 min) | 3.56 | 2.98 |
+| OOM kills | 0 (`max` hits 236,171) | 0 |
+
+**F8 — near-cap pressure is structural to all-seat sittings, not just the default
+cadence.** Event-driven sittings cut invocation *frequency* (the 07:09 trough ran at
+63% of cap), but any sitting that legitimately convenes all ten seats — e.g. a bill
+ballot — spikes straight back to 91–96% of the 8 GiB cap. The savings are real but
+they are frequency savings, not peak savings. Implication for the Act 10 renewal
+debate: event-driven sittings should be kept (frequency/credit win) AND the peak
+question should be answered separately — e.g. does parliament need all ten agents
+concurrent for a ballot, or can ballots be collected from seats as they complete?
+That is an institutional-design question, flagged for the Research Office / Institutional
+innnovator, not for this seat. Renewal evidence so far: throughput up (M-005), credit
+down (frequency), peaks unchanged (this sample). Ballot on file: yes.
+
